  "body": "**No new \u03ba.** This screens the Barnes\u2013Wall lattice BW16 as a unit, using the screen main already applies to the ADE root systems, K12, Leech and the Niemeier lattices (`research/e8-decoder-floors-and-cold14-notes/ade_screen.py`, #620; #598; #606). BW16 has not been screened before. Its ceiling comes out 35% below E8's.\n\n## Change and mathematical scope\n\nOne self-contained package, `research/bw16-unit-screen/`, plus one path-filtered workflow and one `.gitattributes` line. No existing file changes.\n\n- **Lattice.** BW16 = RM(1,4) + 2\u00b7RM(3,4) + 4\u2124\u00b9\u2076 (Construction D). It is rebuilt from code generators. All 4320 minimal vectors are enumerated: the shapes (\u00b12\u00b2) and (\u00b11\u2078) are proved to be the only norm-8 vectors. They give 2160 lines.\n  - Every line has the same \\|\u27e8x, y\u27e9\\| profile: {0: 855, 2: 1024, 4: 280}.\n- **Window.**\n  - `data/clique72.json` holds 72 pairwise non-orthogonal lines. A deterministic greedy reproduces it exactly.\n  - At the decoder pattern of `format-limits` \u00a7C.6 (#446 Theorem A), the clique forces B[K, K] = I, so the window is c \u2265 72. For comparison, E8 has c = 9, and its largest non-orthogonal set is exactly 8, which is also checked.\n  - The pattern's natural decoder B\u2080 has F\u2082 rank 191, so 72 \u2264 c_min \u2264 191.\n- **Screen.**\n  - The screen uses `ade_screen.py`'s proxy x = c\u00b7h/v = 8/15 and #595's certified `skel.X`, imported unchanged.\n  - Result: X(s, 16, 1, 8/15) \u2264 3.8923\u00b710\u207b\u00b3 at every s = 3\u202639. That is 0.649\u00d7 E8's bit ceiling (6.0005\u00b710\u207b\u00b3, reproduced) and below the full-E8 ceiling (5.1598\u00b710\u207b\u00b3).\n- **Break-even.** BW16 loses at every s if and only if c \u2265 63. So the conclusion does not depend on finding the largest clique.\n- **Cause of the loss.** It is entirely the copy charge. With free copies, X(3, 16, 1, 0) = 1.96\u00b710\u207b\u00b2, more than 3\u00d7 E8's ceiling.\n\n**Scope.**\n- This is a ranking screen of the same kind as main's ADE, Leech and Niemeier screens. It is not a \u03ba bound.\n- Only decoders on the \u00a7C.6 pattern are covered. k = 1, and helper cost is not priced (#620 \u00a74). BW16 has 855 orthogonal neighbours per line against E8's 63, which points the same way.\n\n**Duplicates.** Checked against main at `f567884a` and against the titles and bodies of every hub PR through #883: none screens BW16. The Gaussian E8 clique ceilings (#738, #770, #868) concern a different unit.\n\n## Validation\n\n`python3 -B research/bw16-unit-screen/bw16_check.py` uses the standard library only. It prints `PASS_BW16_UNIT_SCREEN (21 checks)` in about 2 min 40 s. It passed on Linux aarch64 with Python 3.12.3 and 3.11.16, against main at `f567884a`. CI runs it on Python 3.11 and 3.14.\n\n## Credits and AI disclosure\n\n- DreamingOfClouds: #595's certified ceiling and the `format-limits` decoder-pattern results.\n- Joel Pulikkan (GamingPuzzled): #620's unit screen.\n- The authors of #446, #598 and #606.\n- BW16 and Construction D are classical (Barnes and Wall 1959; Conway and Sloane, *SPLAG*, ch. 4 and 8).\n- Prepared by DaysSky with assistance from Claude.\n",
